<p>When a building has both kinds of damage, each insurer may try to assign as much as possible to the other policy. Evidence matters: photos of roof and wall openings, the height of water lines inside and out, debris patterns, and the timing of the storm. Our <a href="/wind-vs-flood-damage-port-arthur-insurance/">wind vs. flood damage</a> article and <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a> page explain how these disputes are handled.</p>

<h2>TWIA and flood policies in Sabine Pass</h2>
<p>Sabine Pass is in Jefferson County, a first-tier coastal county in the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association area. Many owners here depend on TWIA for wind coverage. According to <a href="https://www.twia.org/claims/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TWIA</a>, claims must be reported within one year of the date of loss, and TWIA generally has 60 days to accept or reject a claim. Disputes over the amount can go to appraisal, with its own deadline. Repairs, including roof repairs and re-roofs, generally must be inspected under the TDI windstorm program to keep eligibility. See <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a>.</p>
<p>Flood coverage is the other half. NFIP policies typically have a 30-day waiting period, so they need to be in place well before hurricane season. NFIP claims also have their own proof-of-loss requirements. See <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Sabine Pass property types</h2>
<p>Sabine Pass properties include single-family homes, manufactured homes, camps, marine and fishing businesses, and metal buildings. Coastal properties face salt air, high winds, and surge risk, and some are built on elevated foundations. Each raises its own issues: elevated homes can have damage to stairs, decks, and the enclosed space below; metal buildings can suffer panel and fastener failures; and total losses involve questions about contents, debris removal, and additional living expenses. Our <a href="/services/residential-property-claims/">residential</a> and <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial</a> claim pages go into more detail.</p>

